      "title": "Section 3: Engineering Marvels of Ancient Roman Aqueducts",
      "text": "Paragraph A: The Engineering Legacy of Ancient Roman Aqueduct Networks\nWater management and hydraulic engineering reached extraordinary levels of sophistication during the height of the Roman Empire. Spanning thousands of kilometers across continental Europe, North Africa, and the Near East, Roman aqueduct networks conveyed millions of liters of pristine spring water from distant mountain catchments directly into densely populated urban centers. Rather than relying on mechanical pumps or fuel-driven pressure systems, Roman hydraulic architects harnessed gravity alone, constructing continuous masonry channels with delicate, sub-degree slope gradients that maintained constant flow velocity while preventing inner channel erosion.\n\nParagraph B: Structural Innovations in Valley Spans and Subterranean Tunnels\nWhere aqueduct routes encountered deep river valleys and rocky gorges, engineers constructed monumental multi-tiered stone arch bridges, such as the famous Pont du Gard in southern France. These massive structures featured precisely dressed limestone blocks assembled without mortar, held together by interlocking stone joinery and forged iron clamps. Conversely, where terrain permitted, channels were carved deep underground through solid bedrock tunnels. Subterranean routing protected urban water supplies from physical contamination, hostile sabotage, and extreme summer evaporation under intense Mediterranean temperatures.\n\nParagraph C: Urban Storage, Pressurized Distribution, and Municipal Sanitation\nUpon reaching city boundaries, incoming aqueduct channels discharged into large masonry distribution basins known as castella aquae. From these elevated reservoirs, pressurized lead and terracotta pipes distributed water to public fountains, imperial bath complexes, and wealthy private residences. Historical estimates suggest that ancient Rome received over one million cubic meters of fresh water daily\u2014a per capita volume exceeding the municipal water supply of many modern European capitals. Excess water flowing continuously through public baths flushed urban drainage sewers, establishing unprecedented standards of municipal public sanitation.",
